      "text": "We start by determining the direction vector of the line passing through the points $(2,2,1)$ and $(5,1,-2)$. The direction vector $\\mathbf{d}$ can be found by subtracting the coordinates of the first point from the coordinates of the second point:\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{d} = (5-2, 1-2, -2-1) = (3, -1, -3)\n\\]\n\nNext, we use the parametric form of the line equation. The line passing through a point $(x_0, y_0, z_0)$ with direction vector $\\mathbf{d} = (a, b, c)$ can be written as:\n\n\\[\n(x, y, z) = (x_0, y_0, z_0) + t(a, b, c)\n\\]\n\nWe are given that the $x$-coordinate of the point on the line is 4. Let this point be represented by $t = \\frac{x_0 - 4}{a}$. We need to find the value of this point along the line. Substituting $x = 4$ into the parametric form of the line equation, we have:\n\n\\[\n4 = 2 + t \\cdot 3 \\implies 4 = 2 + 3t \\implies 2 = 3t \\implies t = \\frac{2}{3}\n\\]\n\nNow, we substitute $t = \\frac{2}{3}$ into the direction vector components to find the corresponding $y$ and $z$ coordinates in terms of the direction vector components:\n\n\\[\ny = 2 + \\left(\\frac{2}{3} \\cdot -1\\right) = 2 - \\frac{2}{3} = \\frac{6}{3} - \\frac{2}{3} = \\frac{4}{3}\n\\]\n\\[\nz = 1 + \\left(\\frac{2}{3} \\cdot -3\\right) = 1 - 2 = -1\n\\]\n\nThus, the coordinates of the point are $\\left(4, \\frac{4}{3}, -1\\right)$. Therefore, the $z$-coordinate of the point is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{-1}\n\\]",
