      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to find the equation of the line passing through the points \\((2, 2, 1)\\) and \\((5, 1, -2)\\). Once we have the parametric equations of the line, we can use the \\(x\\)-coordinate to find the corresponding \\(z\\)-coordinate.\n\n### Step 1: Find the direction vector of the line\nThe direction vector \\(\\mathbf{d}\\) of the line can be found by subtracting the coordinates of the first point from the coordinates of the second point:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{d} = (5 - 2, 1 - 2, -2 - 1) = (3, -1, -3)\n\\]\n\n### Step 2: Write the parametric equations of the line\nThe parametric equations of the line can be written using the point \\((2, 2, 1)\\) and the direction vector \\((3, -1, -3)\\):\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\nx = 2 + 3t \\\\\ny = 2 - t \\\\\nz = 1 - 3t\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nwhere \\(t\\) is a parameter.\n\n### Step 3: Find the \\(z\\)-coordinate for \\(x = 4\\)\nWe need to find the value of \\(t\\) when \\(x = 4\\). Substitute \\(x = 4\\) into the parametric equation for \\(x\\):\n\\[\n4 = 2 + 3t\n\\]\nSolving for \\(t\\):\n\\[\n4 - 2 = 3t \\implies 2 = 3t \\implies t = \\frac{2}{3}\n\\]\n\n### Step 4: Substitute \\(t = \\frac{2}{3}\\) into the parametric equation for \\(z\\)\nNow that we have \\(t = \\frac{2}{3}\\), substitute this value into the parametric equation for \\(z\\):\n\\[\nz = 1 - 3t = 1 - 3 \\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right) = 1 - 2 = -1\n\\]\n\nThus, the \\(z\\)-coordinate of the point on the line when the \\(x\\)-coordinate is 4 is \\(\\boxed{-1}\\).",
