      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find the \\( z \\)-coordinate of the point on the line that passes through \\((2,2,1)\\) and \\((5,1,-2)\\) with an \\( x \\)-coordinate of 4, we start by determining the parametric equations of the line. The general form of the parametric equations of a line through two points \\((x_1, y_1, z_1)\\) and \\((x_2, y_2, z_2)\\) with parameter \\( t \\) is given by:\n\\[\nx = x_1 + t(x_2 - x_1), \\quad y = y_1 + t(y_2 - y_1), \\quad z = z_1 + t(z_2 - z_1)\n\\]\nHere, \\((x_1, y_1, z_1) = (2, 2, 1)\\) and \\((x_2, y_2, z_2) = (5, 1, -2)\\). Substituting these values into the parametric equations, we get:\n\\[\nx = 2 + t(5 - 2) = 2 + 3t, \\quad y = 2 + t(1 - 2) = 2 - t, \\quad z = 1 + t(-2 - 1) = 1 - 3t\n\\]\nWe are given that the \\( x \\)-coordinate of the point is 4. So, we substitute \\( x = 4 \\) into the equation for \\( x \\):\n\\[\n4 = 2 + 3t\n\\]\nSolving for \\( t \\), we get:\n\\[\n4 - 2 = 3t \\implies 2 = 3t \\implies t = \\frac{2}{3}\n\\]\nNow that we have \\( t = \\frac{2}{3} \\), we substitute this value into the equation for \\( z \\) to find the \\( z \\)-coordinate:\n\\[\nz = 1 - 3\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right) = 1 - 2 = -1\n\\]\nTherefore, the \\( z \\)-coordinate of the point is \\(\\boxed{-1}\\).",
